== 1. The Use and Application of All

The use and application of all follows.

Is it true that closet prayer or private prayer is such an indispensable duty, one that Christ Himself has laid on all who are not willing to lie under the woeful brand of being hypocrites? Then this truth looks very sourly and sadly on these five sorts of persons.

=== a. Those Who Put Off Secret Prayer Until They Are Moved to It by the Spirit

First, it looks sourly and sadly on all those who put off secret prayer, private prayer, until they are moved to it by the Spirit; for by this sad delusion many have been kept from secret prayer many weeks, many months—oh, that I did not have to say it—many years! Though it is a very fit season to pray when the Spirit moves us to pray, yet it is not the only season to pray (Isa 62:1; Ps 123:1–2; Gal 4:6). He who makes religion his business will pray daily for daily grace as surely as he prays daily for daily bread. “Then Jesus told them a parable about their need to pray at all times and not lose heart” (Luke 18:1). “Pray without ceasing” (1 Thess 5:17). “Pray in the Spirit always, with every kind of prayer and supplication. To this end, stay alert with all perseverance in your#super[+] prayers for all the saints” (Eph 6:18). “Persistent in prayer” (Rom 12:12). The Greek is a metaphor taken from hunting dogs that never give up the chase until they have caught their prey. A Christian must not only pray but hold on in prayer until he has won the heavenly prize. We are always in need; and therefore we need to be praying always. The world is always alluring; and therefore we need to be always praying. Satan is always tempting; and therefore we need to be always praying. And we are always sinning; and therefore we need to be always praying. And we are in dangers always; and therefore we need to be praying always. And we are dying always (1 Cor 15:31); and therefore we need to be praying always. Man’s whole life is only a lingering death. No sooner does man begin to live than he begins to die. When one man was asked why he prayed six times a day, he gave only this answer: “I must die, I must die, I must die.” Dying Christians need to be praying Christians, and those who are always dying need to be always praying. Certainly, prayerless families are graceless families, and prayerless persons are graceless persons (Jer 10:25). It would be ten thousand times better that we had never been born into the world than that we should go stillborn out of the world.

=== b. Those Who Do Not Pray at All, Either in Their Families or in Their Closets

Secondly, this truth looks sourly and sadly on those who do not pray at all, either in their families or in their closets. Among all God’s children, there is not one possessed with a mute devil. Prayerless persons are forsaken by God, blinded by Satan, hardened in sin, and with every breath they draw are liable to all temporal, spiritual, and eternal judgments. Prayer is that part of natural worship due to God which no one will deny but out-and-out atheists (Ps 14:1).

It is worth noting that among the worst of men, the Turks, and among the worst of the Turks, the Moors, it is by their law a just objection against any witness that he has not prayed six times in every full day, for it is usual with them to pray six times a day.

(1) Before daybreak they pray for the day to come.

(2) When it is day, they give thanks for the day.

(3) At noon, they thank God for half the day past.

(4) After that, they pray for a good sunset.

(5) And after that, they thank God for the day past.

(6) And then, sixthly and lastly, they pray for a good night after their day.

Certainly, these very Moors will one day rise in judgment against those who cast off prayer, who live in a total neglect of prayer, who allow so many suns and moons to rise and set over their heads without any solemn calling on God. I have read of a man who, being sick and afraid of death, fell to his prayers, and to move God to hear him, told Him that he was no common beggar, that he had never troubled Him with his prayers before, and that if He would only hear him this time, he would never trouble Him again. This world is full of such profane, blasphemous, atheistic wretches.

=== c. Those Who Are All for Public Prayer, but Never Regard Private Prayer

Thirdly, this truth looks very sourly and sadly on those who are all for public prayer but never regard private prayer, who are all for going up to the temple but never care about going into their closets. It is the most obvious hypocrisy for a man to be very zealous for public prayer but very cold and careless about private prayer. He who claims to be conscientious in the one and does not make a matter of conscience of the other is a hypocrite through and through (Matt 23:5; 6:1, 2, 5). And the devil knows well enough how to make his profit from all such hypocrites, who are all for the prayers of the church but care nothing for private prayer, like Gallio (Acts 18:17). Those who perform all their private devotion in the church but not in their room show too great a disregard for the authority of Christ, who says, “When you pray, go into your closet.” He does not say, “When you pray, go to the church,” but, “When you pray, go into your closet.”

=== d. Those Who in Their Closets Pray with a Loud, Clamorous Voice

Fourthly, this truth looks sadly and sourly on those who in their closets pray with a loud, clamorous voice. A Christian should shut both the door of his closet and the door of his lips so tight that no one outside should hear what he says inside. “Go into your closet,” says Christ, “shut your door, and pray” (Matt 6:6). But why need a man shut his closet door if he may pray with a clamorous voice, if he makes such a noise that everyone in the street or everyone in the house may hear him? The hen, when she lays her eggs, gets into a hole, a corner; but then she makes such a noise with her cackling that she tells everyone in the house where she is and what she is about. Christians who imitate the hen in their closets pray rather to be seen, heard, and observed by men than out of any noble design to glorify God, or to pour out their souls before Him who sees in secret. Sometimes children, when they are vexed or afraid of the rod, will run behind the door or get into a dark hole, and there they will lie crying, and sighing, and sobbing, so that the whole house may know where they are.

Oh, it is a childish thing so to cry, and sigh, and sob in our closets as to tell everyone in the house where we are and what work we are about. Well, Christians, for an effectual remedy of this evil, frequently and seriously consider these five things:

First, that God sees in secret.

Secondly, that God has a quick ear, and is taken more with the voice of the heart than with the clamor of the mouth. God can easily hear the most secret breathings of your soul. God is more attentive in observing the messages delivered by the heart than those that are only delivered by the mouth. He who prays aloud in private seems to tell others that God does not understand the secret desires, and thoughts, and workings of His people’s hearts.

Thirdly, it is not fitting, it is not suitable nor beneficial, that anyone should be acquainted with our secret prayers but God and our own souls. Now it is as much our duty to look to what is beneficial as it is to look to what is permissible (2 Cor 8:10). “Everything is permissible for me, but not everything is beneficial” (1 Cor 6:12). “Everything is permissible, but not everything is beneficial. Everything is permissible, but not everything is edifying” (1 Cor 10:23). Now it is so far from being beneficial that it is very great folly for men to lay open their secret infirmities to others, who will sooner mock them than lift up a prayer for them.

Fourthly, loud prayers may be a hindrance and disturbance to others who may be busy near us in some religious or secular exercises.

Fifthly and lastly, Hannah prayed and yet spoke not a word. Her heart was full, but her voice was not heard (1 Sam 1:13). Moses prays and cries and yet lets fall not a word. “Then the #smallcaps[Lord] said to Moses, ‘Why are you crying out to Me?’” (Exod 14:15). Moses did not cry with any audible voice, but with inward sighs, and secret breathings, and wrestlings of soul. And these inward and secret cries, which made no noise, carried the day with God; for Moses is heard and answered, and his people are delivered. Oh, the prevailing power of those prayers that make no noise in the ears of others!

=== e. Those Who Hinder and Discourage Others from This Duty of Duties

Fifthly and lastly, this truth looks sourly and sadly on those who do all they can to #emph[hinder and discourage others from this duty] of duties, private prayer, either by mocking or vilifying the duty, or by denying that it is a duty, or by their own daily neglect of this duty, or by denying those under them time and opportunity for the discharge of this duty. In Matthew 23:13 you#super[+] have a woe pronounced against those who will neither go to heaven themselves nor allow others to go who are willing to enter into an everlasting rest. And so I say, Woe to those parents, and woe to those husbands, and woe to those masters and mistresses, who will neither pray in their closets themselves nor allow their children, nor their wives, nor their servants to pour out their souls before the Lord in a corner. Oh, sirs! How will you#super[+] answer this to your#super[+] consciences when you#super[+] lie on a dying bed? And how will you#super[+] answer it to the Judge of all the world when you#super[+] stand before a judgment seat? Certainly, all their sins, and all their neglects, and all their spiritual losses that might have been prevented by their secret prayers, by their closet communion with God, will one day be charged to your#super[+] account. And oh, that you#super[+] were all so wise as to lay these things to heart, so that you#super[+] may never again hinder any who are under your#super[+] care or charge from private prayer!

But secondly, this may serve to exhort us to keep close to our closets, to be frequent and constant in private prayer, to be often with God in a corner. The twenty considerations already laid down may serve as so many motives to stir up your#super[+] hearts to this noble and necessary duty.
